  "batch_notes": "Three shapes worth flagging. (1) Performance/DoS properties (pulldown-cmark x3) assert a linear event-count bound and a wall-clock bound and use tc.target(elapsed) to steer toward slow inputs; the rubric's robustness ('no hang') is the nearest fit but a 'complexity bound' notion would describe them better. (2) 'Round trip under chunking' (postcard accumulator, prost split-buffer decode) sits between roundtrip and internal-consistency (chunked vs whole); I put both under roundtrip because the assertion is against the original input, not against a whole-buffer decode. (3) Several registered properties are not generative at all: pretty-bytes doc_examples and negative_zero_is_zero, and printf-compat doc_examples take a TestCase and never draw from it; they are example tests in property clothing and will inflate property counts. Two target families (printf-compat, pyproject-toml) consist mostly of one broad oracle property plus one narrowed pin-property per bug, all with `property` already set on the bug record, so pin-tagged properties carry most of the direct bug attributions there. Reference-via-stdlib cases (piccolo float ops vs Rust f64, priority-queue sorted vec vs std sort) were classified external-oracle per the rubric's stdlib clause, though they are borderline with model-oracle. piccolo's load_deeply_nested_source_never_aborts had to cap depth at 100 because hegel's own stack use makes the crate's MAX_RECURSION guard insufficient; the real overflows live in an #[ignore]d pin that is not in this batch's pins, so piccolo/1 has no pin record here. The postcard README lists a varint_roundtrip_props! macro family that is absent from the batch's properties (macro-generated tests may not be extracted)."
}
